#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133574 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ae774f-2496-56c0-a800-450bd8383092" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Inner Product of Two Complex Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "3263f466-0901-58a7-8cd9-ed62cd3036a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5545" :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf y\\in\\mathbb C^3,$ compute the standard Hermitian inner product $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle$ of the following two vectors.\n\n![](images/d3/d3c0e32110f0ff3ffe91363297c0a4242b60b12aa9963653da0608d664719826.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}} \\\\  & =i\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-3})+(-2)\\cdot \\overset{―}{4+i}+0\\cdot \\overset{―}{5} \\\\  & =i\\cdot (-3)+(-2)\\cdot (4-i)+0 \\\\  & =-3i-8+2i \\\\  & =-8-i.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133575 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/id #uuid "b30bd921-7418-5a57-96eb-0c79dd5e4712" :question/math-academy-id "e-5547" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $\\langle \\,\\cdot\\,, \\,\\cdot\\, \\rangle$ over $\\mathbb{C}.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle\\in\\mathbb R$ for any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$\n\n- $\\langle \\mathrm{i}\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle =  \\langle \\mathbf{a},  -\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{b} \\rangle$ for any  $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$\n\n- $\\langle\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a},\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number for any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\alpha \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\alpha \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} + \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\alpha\\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\alpha} \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number, and $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = 0$ if and only if $\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the linearity in the first argument  and the antilinearity in the second argument, we have\n\n$$\n\\langle  \\mathrm{i}\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle =  \\mathrm{i}\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle  = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\overline{\\, \\mathrm{i}\\, } \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a},- \\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{b} \\rangle\n$$\n\n \nfor any  $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, by the linearity in the first argument  and the antilinearity in the second argument, we have\n\n$$\n\\langle\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a},\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a} \\rangle =\\mathrm{i}\\cdot \\bar{ \\mathrm{i} }\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = -\\mathrm{i}^2\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle= \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle\n$$\n\n \nfor any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n.$ But by positive-definiteness, this is a nonnegative real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133681 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133865 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/id #uuid "8d5f83f0-dfb9-5437-a0e5-f33f68a59cec" :question/math-academy-id "e-8578" :question/problem "Given that $\\langle \\,\\cdot\\,, \\,\\cdot\\, \\rangle$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $\\mathbb{C},$ find $\\big\\langle -2\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}, \\: \\mathbf{c} \\big\\rangle$ if \n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = 3+5\\text{i}, \\quad \\quad \\langle \\mathbf{c}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = 2-4\\text{i}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\alpha \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\alpha \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} + \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\alpha\\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\alpha} \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number, and $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = 0$ if and only if $\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ is linear in the first argument: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨-2a+3b,\\,c⟩ & =⟨-2a,c⟩+⟨3b,c⟩ \\\\  & =-2⟨a,c⟩+3⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2⟨a,c⟩+3⟨b,c⟩ & =-2⟨a,c⟩+3\\overset{―}{⟨c,b⟩}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle$ and $\\langle \\mathbf{c}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2⟨a,c⟩+3\\overset{―}{⟨c,b⟩} & =-2(3+5\\text{i})+3\\overset{―}{(2-4\\text{i})} \\\\  & =-2(3+5\\text{i})+3(2+4\\text{i}) \\\\  & =-6-10\\text{i}+6+12\\text{i} \\\\  & =2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ae774f-2496-56c0-a800-450bd8383092" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Inner Product of Two Complex Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04cb1513-0494-55b4-ac21-1426d212719b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11-4i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326983} {:answer/id #uuid "4b417682-c280-52c3-930e-bd28bd16b274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12-4i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327593} {:answer/id #uuid "6334eda9-72fd-5ec6-9bb6-45a2d8a5acca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14+i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327800} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7392f4-a493-50bb-be49-342db1acbba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328299} {:answer/id #uuid "b40650a4-055b-56af-af39-3c88f7bd4955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10-2i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04cb1513-0494-55b4-ac21-1426d212719b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11-4i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326983} :answer-field/id #uuid "18e7f2cb-d67a-58bf-a1a4-0e5118385e11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59f23fa6-6c50-54ee-b500-91f27bbfcf52" :question/math-academy-id "q-43804" :question/problem "Given that $x,y\\in {C}^{3},$ compute the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨x,y⟩$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1+i \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2-i\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}1+i \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2+i\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}} \\\\  & =(1+i)\\cdot (\\overset{―}{1+i})+2\\cdot \\overset{―}{3}+(2-i)(\\overset{―}{2+\\text{i}}) \\\\  & =(1+i)\\cdot (1-i)+2\\cdot 3+(2-i)(2-\\text{i}) \\\\  & =2+6+(3-4\\text{i}) \\\\  & =11-4i.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329734 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ae774f-2496-56c0-a800-450bd8383092" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Inner Product of Two Complex Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55a29361-ace2-5448-9e8a-56004d801265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327674} {:answer/id #uuid "8af60669-f3a9-50a6-8020-dd0b740bf3f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328127} {:answer/id #uuid "8f75554c-ae16-5ab0-8dbb-8a16a57dbb98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1+2i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328186} {:answer/id #uuid "9e26d74f-3385-50c6-a4b9-096354944b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-2i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328297} {:answer/id #uuid "bdf311fc-e039-561a-a505-b935875771e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55a29361-ace2-5448-9e8a-56004d801265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327674} :answer-field/id #uuid "937634a1-c04b-5094-b4f7-46fd86cf02b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "54af8153-1ada-54d5-a9ec-29f6e18d959b" :question/math-academy-id "q-43808" :question/problem "Given that $x,y\\in {C}^{3},$ compute the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨x,y⟩$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}i \\\\ 1+2i \\\\ -i\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}1+i \\\\ -1 \\\\ -i\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}} \\\\  & =i\\cdot (\\overset{―}{1+i})+(1+2i)\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-1})+(-i)\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-\\text{i}}) \\\\  & =i\\cdot (1-i)+(1+2i)\\cdot (-1)+(-i)\\cdot \\text{i} \\\\  & =(1+i)+(-1-2i)+1 \\\\  & =1-i.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134549 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133865 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b922b8f-90f0-5bd8-9404-ff43b4baa638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358401} {:answer/id #uuid "658ae874-b297-562b-8a62-02be270bace9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11+9\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358641} {:answer/id #uuid "919c7333-f6e4-5696-ad9e-7839ff2b1918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7+9\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359031} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c510ab-f8e0-5db9-83cb-6243d23e13d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359506} {:answer/id #uuid "ed361081-0bf2-59ed-8dcd-4cbf9ba2f041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7c510ab-f8e0-5db9-83cb-6243d23e13d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359506} :answer-field/id #uuid "83e90b7a-7c25-529e-9722-30e5c26dc7c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "807cae0b-c171-5b3d-8945-284698d35162" :question/math-academy-id "q-96814" :question/problem "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨5a-2b,\\,c⟩$ if \n\n$\\,$ $⟨c,a⟩=-1+\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨b,c⟩=3-2\\text{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is linear in the first argument: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨5a-2b,\\,c⟩ & =⟨5a,c⟩-⟨2b,c⟩ \\\\  & =5⟨a,c⟩-2⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5⟨a,c⟩-2⟨b,c⟩ & =5\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}-2⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨c,a⟩$ and $⟨b,c⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}-2⟨b,c⟩ & =5(\\overset{―}{-1+\\text{i}})-2(3-2\\text{i}) \\\\  & =5(-1-\\text{i})-2(3-2\\text{i}) \\\\  & =-5-5\\text{i}-6+4\\text{i} \\\\  & =-11-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360771 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133865 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f249c59-b32c-5e1f-8a73-39662bb8292d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8-12\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357874} {:answer/id #uuid "43b90f1b-a8a0-5f06-b237-83d19006453d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12+8\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358330} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ac3ea0-e312-5e28-8c23-356c622cff14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8+12\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359462} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e924fb-c801-5b22-b555-7971eaefc39f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359511} {:answer/id #uuid "dcfdd954-f242-5cf2-9b95-285af5bec4fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f249c59-b32c-5e1f-8a73-39662bb8292d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8-12\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357874} :answer-field/id #uuid "69c9247f-1770-53ee-bce2-d83e61f133f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "589fd79e-dcf6-5066-9979-9da6a025b573" :question/math-academy-id "q-97215" :question/problem "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨6a+4b,\\,c⟩$ if \n\n$\\,$ $⟨c,a⟩=2+4\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨c,b⟩=-5-3\\text{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is linear in the first argument: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨6a+4b,\\,c⟩ & =⟨6a,c⟩+⟨4b,c⟩ \\\\  & =6⟨a,c⟩+4⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6⟨a,c⟩+4⟨b,c⟩ & =6\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}+4\\overset{―}{⟨c,b⟩}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨c,a⟩$ and $⟨c,b⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}+4\\overset{―}{⟨c,b⟩} & =6(\\overset{―}{2+4\\text{i}})+4\\overset{―}{(-5-3\\text{i})} \\\\  & =6(2-4\\text{i})+4(-5+3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =12-24\\text{i}-20+12\\text{i} \\\\  & =-8-12\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1104a8cb-ad84-52f3-b50b-876c66edd164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357895} {:answer/id #uuid "2329b63f-d510-5ea2-bd87-da0ae0af0541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358061} {:answer/id #uuid "808dcaba-7678-52d3-b0de-9a21f22bf7a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358874} {:answer/id #uuid "d3cb4f20-39fc-57f8-9567-f4d56068222a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359602} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ac94e8-e5cb-5eee-8322-0e8dc9ec51e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1104a8cb-ad84-52f3-b50b-876c66edd164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357895} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f10023a-ece8-528b-83fb-f11650f78eba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d89a9f6-d33c-56fa-8ee8-b25b137af49f" :question/math-academy-id "q-97264" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,\\overset{―}{\\alpha }b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n- $⟨a,a⟩>0$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement II is true. By the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument, we have \n\n$$\n⟨a,\\overset{―}{\\alpha }b⟩=\\overset{―}{(\\overset{―}{\\alpha })}⟨a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $a=0$ then $⟨a,a⟩=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cd595f9-17e4-5a1b-a94e-048841136360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358977} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d462ad-5b75-5127-bb94-4089c68fb413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359287} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c1b85d-e756-5311-9ca3-541dea10a922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359325} {:answer/id #uuid "d6e41ff6-6d42-5bf8-b72f-6c6339c8006f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359632} {:answer/id #uuid "ef82daa4-23f9-582e-8349-bfa483e02f57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6e41ff6-6d42-5bf8-b72f-6c6339c8006f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359632} :answer-field/id #uuid "44c0a61a-778b-5fba-a1df-8ed742b7e29b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8247f7d2-00cd-54b9-9bd1-43c13383de3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-97303" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨b,a⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨a,b⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $i⟨a,b⟩=⟨-ia,b⟩$ for any  $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement II is false. A complex  inner product is linear  in the first argument,  so we have \n\n$$\n⟨-ia,b⟩=-i⟨a,b⟩\\ne i⟨a,b⟩,\n$$\n\nin general.\n\n- Statement III is true. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$  $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}]}
